WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:00.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  from the very opening scene of the  batman new villain the riddler enacts a  murderous and effective campaign of  terror to expose the corruption at the  heart of gotham city the frightening  villain first targets city officials  while leaving a series of riddles chock  full of clues for batman to uncover the  conspiracy that has turned gotham rotten  in fact riddler later tells an appalled  batman that he was inspired by the caped  crusader's own use of violence and  crafting his plans to permanently change  the city and its infrastructure the  riddler is eventually captured but  batman is too late to work out the final  part of his plan after the riddler's car  bombs destroy the breakwaters of gotham  flooding the downtown area his followers  intend to kill mayor-elect bella royale  and shooting to the crowd of people  taking refuge in the stadium batman and  catwoman are forced to fight the  riddler's devotees and save the city  residents fans praised how the early  stages of the riddler's master plan felt  but were very split over his scheme in  the last act here's why in the  discussion of the batman and the r movie  subreddit some superhero fans were  frustrated by how nonsensical the  riddler's endgame is compared to what  one user called quote his brilliant plan  to publicly embarrass expose and  assassinate the city's leadership  earlier in the movie the riddler did  tell his devotees that he wanted real  change and acted in gotham but creating  a major disaster via bombing gotham sea  walls and then using that flood to kill  mayor-elect bella real is not on par  with the string of killings riddler  mastermind earlier in the movie the same  redditor also noticed that the mayhem  didn't even make sense they wrote the  plan was to get everyone in the stadium  and have like 10 guys in masks snipe  everyone why not just blow up the  building the scheme seems like it'd be  too easy for someone like batman to foil  compared to the destructive power of a  bomb or another device it's  understandable then that some fans took  issue with the riddler expanding his  lethal crusade to ordinary citizens  instead of just corrupt city officials  part of the issue is that unlike batman  begins which involves the villain  tampering with gotham's water supply in  the final act the destruction of  gotham's sea wall isn't foreshadowed or  telegraphed to the audience at all one  reddit user noticed that writing at no  point in the movie are we ever told  about a sea wall keeping gotham dry  suddenly the riddler is blowing up dikes  to drown the city and wash everything  away it's worth noting that there were  some passing references to the sea wall  like hidden on riddler's floor map and  in newspaper clippings so you would have  had to pay attention to the small  details to figure out the clues the  flooding also doesn't feel consistent  with the tone of the dark stripped-down  film compared to the detective story at  the heart of the batman the last 30  minutes are more of the climax to what  feels like a standard action-packed  superhero blockbuster one reddit user  wrote that in their opinion the final  act was needlessly dramatic compared to  the rest of the movie that felt pretty  grounded until that point however other  redditors pointed out that the final  scenes helped complete batman's arc  throughout the batman the titular hero  often comes off more like a movie  monster than a superhero batman is  obsessed with the gotham project and as  bella real observes isn't doing much to  help the population when he's bruce  wayne you really could be doing more for  this city  your family has a history of  philanthropy but as far as i can tell  you're not doing anything but when  batman saves the innocent folks trapped  in the flooded downtown arena this leads  mayor real and the helpless citizens to  trust him enough to lead them out of the  flood he also recognizes that the  riddler shooters are traumatized angry  people much like himself one redditer  argued that seeing batman evolve as a  hero through those scenes was crucial  additionally bruce empathizes with the  people he rescues as well as the  shooters in the end batman decides that  he can't only personify vengeance but  should also be an inspiration to the  residents of gotham who just survived a  terrifying event arguably the batman  could have had a different climax but it  might not have delivered the same  emotional payoff  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

